  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Friday)\nIssued at 144 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nFor the start of the new week, most model agreement does see the \nupper-level high pressure system stay seated over the Southwestern\nUntied States. However, as the upper-level trough to our east \nbegins to exit further east, most runs do see the high start to \nelongate back eastwards towards the Panhandles. This elongation \nwill in turn see the Panhandles warm up once again with most of \nthe week seeing high temperatures near or over the triple-digit \nmark. Monday in particular could see some of our hottest \ntemperatures with current trends suggesting a possible need for \nheat related products. What may inhibit the spread of this heat \nMonday, however, is the presence of a weak short-wave that may \nallow for afternoon showers and thunderstorms across the west. In \nfact these chances of afternoon activity looks to be a theme for \nthe most of next week with models either suggesting a new short- \nwave staring activity or storm motion being strong enough to push \nactivity off the mountains. Still these chances are on the low end\nat around 10 to 20 percent with potential for any meaningful \nrainfall evening lower than that.",
